      "brief": "Tata Motors raised its all‑cash voluntary tender offer for Iveco to €14.40 per share, valuing the deal at €3.91 billion (33.47% premium to the July 17, 2025 price and 6.77% to the July 29, 2025 price). The increase is funded partly by an €85 million credit facility with MUFG Bank and Tata says the price will not be raised further.",
      "enough_text": true
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "brief": "Tata Motors’ €3.91‑billion bid for Iveco is in its final phase; the “best and final” €14.40 per share offer must secure enough shareholder tenders, with Exor’s 27.06% stake supporting the deal and three investors holding about 20% yet to tender, making the 80% acceptance threshold critical.",
      "enough_text": true
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "brief": "Stellantis India, Tata Motors Passenger Vehicles and its electric subsidiary signed a definitive agreement expanding their partnership to advanced vehicle technology and product collaboration, using Tata’s architecture and Stellantis’ engineering to develop vehicles in India for more than 50 global markets, to be built at the 50:50 FIAPL joint venture in Pune.",
      "enough_text": true
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "brief": "Tata Motors Passenger Vehicles and Stellantis formalised an expanded partnership that combines Tata’s vehicle architecture with Stellantis’ global product development, targeting vehicles for over 50 markets and manufacturing in India via the FIAPL joint venture, building on a February 2026 MoU.",
      "enough_text": true
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "brief": "Tata Motors increased its Iveco offer to €14.40 per share (a 2% rise) after activist hedge fund Petrus Advisers rejected the prior price; the three largest hold‑out investors—Davidson Kempner, Syquant Capital and Petrus Advisers—control roughly 20.4% of Iveco shares, while Exor holds 27.06% and supports the deal.",
      "enough_text": true
    }
  ],
  "key_points": [
    {
      "text": "Tata Motors raised its takeover offer for Iveco to €14.40 per share, valuing the transaction at €3.91 billion, a 33.47% premium to the July 17, 2025 price and 6.77% premium to the July 29, 2025 price.",
      "citations": [1,5]
    },
    {
      "text": "The revised offer is Tata Motors’ best and final price and will be funded in part by an €85 million credit facility with MUFG Bank; no further increase is planned.",
